From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 17:11:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(allwinner): A733: native PSCI CPU_SUSPEND

Without an SCP, plat_setup_psci_ops() installs sunxi_native_psci_ops,
which declares neither pwr_domain_suspend nor validate_power_state, so
psci_setup() never grants PSCI_CPU_SUSPEND_AARCH64 and every
CPU_SUSPEND answers SMC_UNK. Add a native core-level suspend for the
A733: no new firmware, no SCP, no blob.

THE HARDWARE. SUNXI_R_CPUCFG_BASE (0x07050000) is the A733 UM's
"CPUIDLE" block, whose registers the UM does not describe. Read on
silicon it holds Arm PPU v1.1 components that identify themselves (CIDR
0xB105F00D, part 0x0B6, JEP106 Arm, IIDR 0x0B61143B, AIDR 0x11), so the
register fields come from Arm DEN0051E and the wake path from the Arm
GIC-600 TRM. Instance assignment is stimulus-proven: DSU PPU at
+0x1000, core n at +0x2000 + n * 0x1000.

WHY IT NEEDS ALMOST NO CODE. The cores carry PWR_DYN_EN = 1, and with
dynamic transitions PWR_POLICY is the MINIMUM power mode (DEN0051E
5.2.1). Cores 1-7 sit at PWR_POLICY = OFF, so the hardware may gate them
the moment they drop their P-Channel ON request, which is what
CPUPWRCTLR_EL1.CORE_PWRDN_EN + WFI does. Core 0 was released by boot0
and reads PWR_POLICY = ON; sunxi_cpu_suspend_setup() writes OFF for the
boot core once, read-modify-write, with a read-back.

THE BOOT CORE'S RESET STATE. A core the PPU turns back on starts at its
reset vector; RVBARADDR is that vector only in AArch64 state, and the
state is CLU0_CPUn_CTRL_REG.AA64nAA32 (UM V1.00 5.1.6.6, reset 0 =
AArch32), sampled at reset. sunxi_cpu_on() sets it for the core it
starts, but the boot core never goes through it (silicon: CPU0 0x0,
CPU1-7 0x1). Its first power-down wake ran the BROM in the middle of
the running kernel ("HELLO! BOOT0 is starting!" 1.22 s into Linux, then
RCU stalls). plat_setup_psci_ops() now sets the bit for every core,
next to RVBAR, wherever the platform's cpucfg header defines
SUNXI_CPUCFG_CPU_CTRL_REG. The suspend setup reads both back and
refuses the state to a core whose reset would not land in BL31.

THE GIC. The generic GIC driver does exactly the GIC-600 TRM 3.6.2
sequence around the suspend: gicv3_cpuif_disable() before
pwr_domain_suspend (ProcessorSleep = 1, so the redistributor raises
wake_request; the redistributor stays powered) and gicv3_cpuif_enable()
after pwr_domain_suspend_finish (no gicv3_rdistif_init(), whose
defaults would disable every SGI and PPI, the arch timer among them).
SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND therefore requires USE_GIC_DRIVER.

WHAT IS GATED. validate_power_state decodes the power level (the
StateID is 0) and accepts the core level only. sunxi_cpu_can_suspend()
re-reads the core's PWPR on every CPU_SUSPEND and refuses the state
unless PWR_DYN_EN is set and PWR_POLICY is OFF, unless the PPU's own
IDR0 advertises STA_OFF_SPT and DYN_OFF_SPT, and unless the core
resets into BL31. A core that does not match loses deep idle; it does
not hang.

THE WFI. The PSCI library executes the WFI after pwr_domain_suspend. If
it returned, the library would call the CPU's power-down hook again to
unwind and panic unless the hook acknowledges a powerdown abandon
(psci_pwrdown_cpu_end_wakeup(), prepare_cpu_pwr_up()). The A55 and A76
cannot abandon: with CPUPWRCTLR_EL1.CORE_PWRDN_EN set, a WFI masks all
interrupts and wake-up events and only a reset ends it (Arm Cortex-A55
TRM 100442_0200_03_en, 2.4.8, p. 44-45; Arm Cortex-A76 TRM
100798_0401_01_en, 5.9, p. 44-45), and cortex_a55_core_pwr_dwn() and
cortex_a76_core_pwr_dwn() set that bit and issue an ISB before PSCI
reaches the WFI. A wake event that is already pending takes the core
down and straight back up through reset, the same warm boot as any other
wake; the unwind path is not reached.

EL3 STACKS. The common default, 0x1000 shared by eight cores, is 512 B
per core; the deepest chain from the SMC handler through CPU_SUSPEND
to a print is 928 B (gcc -fcallgraph-info=su on the link-time optimised
release build), and the stacks are contiguous. SUNXI_PLAT_STACK_SIZE
sets a per-core size, and the A733 uses 2048.

The boot core prints one line at cold boot:
  PSCI: native CPU_SUSPEND armed 0xff, core 0 PWPR 00000108->00000100,
  reset to BL31 0xff

Silicon (8 GiB board): all eight cores power down and wake
(2026-09-23); an 8 h load/idle soak with ~2.8 M power-downs and zero
errors (2026-09-24); idle wall power -0.162 W against WFI only.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
 plat/allwinner/common/allwinner-common.mk     |  25 ++
 plat/allwinner/common/include/platform_def.h  |   8 +
 plat/allwinner/common/include/sunxi_private.h |  11 +
 plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_native_pm.c       | 118 +++++++++
 plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_pm.c              |  24 +-
 plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733/platform.mk        |  12 +
 .../allwinner/sun60i_a733/sunxi_cpu_suspend.c | 233 ++++++++++++++++++
 7 files changed, 430 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733/sunxi_cpu_suspend.c

diff --git a/plat/allwinner/common/allwinner-common.mk b/plat/allwinner/common/allwinner-common.mk
index 248933332..adff56aa1 100644
--- a/plat/allwinner/common/allwinner-common.mk
+++ b/plat/allwinner/common/allwinner-common.mk
@@ -62,6 +62,31 @@ endif
 BL31_SOURCES		+=	${AW_PLAT}/common/sunxi_native_pm.c
 endif
 
+# Native PSCI CPU_SUSPEND (core-level power-down without an SCP). Off by
+# default: it needs a per-SoC sunxi_cpu_suspend.c that knows how that SoC lets
+# a core power itself down and be woken again, and getting that wrong leaves a
+# core in WFI that nothing can wake. It leaves the GIC to the generic driver,
+# which the PSCI library calls around the suspend. A platform opts in by
+# setting this to 1 BEFORE including this file.
+SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND	?=	0
+$(call assert_boolean,SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND)
+$(eval $(call add_define,SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND))
+
+ifeq (${SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND},1)
+ifeq (${SUNXI_PSCI_USE_NATIVE},0)
+$(error "SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND needs SUNXI_PSCI_USE_NATIVE")
+endif
+ifeq (${USE_GIC_DRIVER},0)
+$(error "SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND needs USE_GIC_DRIVER")
+endif
+BL31_SOURCES		+=	${AW_PLAT}/${PLAT}/sunxi_cpu_suspend.c
+endif
+
+# EL3 stack per core in bytes, 0 = the default 0x1000 / PLATFORM_CORE_COUNT.
+SUNXI_PLAT_STACK_SIZE		?=	0
+$(call assert_numeric,SUNXI_PLAT_STACK_SIZE)
+$(eval $(call add_define,SUNXI_PLAT_STACK_SIZE))
+
 ifeq (${SUNXI_PSCI_USE_SCPI},1)
 ifneq (${USE_GIC_DRIVER},0)
 $(error "SUNXI_PSCI_USE_SCPI drives the GICv2 itself and cannot be used with USE_GIC_DRIVER")
diff --git a/plat/allwinner/common/include/platform_def.h b/plat/allwinner/common/include/platform_def.h
index 156c06547..e4ef396fa 100644
--- a/plat/allwinner/common/include/platform_def.h
+++ b/plat/allwinner/common/include/platform_def.h
@@ -72,7 +72,15 @@
 #define PLATFORM_CLUSTER_COUNT		U(1)
 #define PLATFORM_CORE_COUNT		(PLATFORM_CLUSTER_COUNT * \
 					 PLATFORM_MAX_CPUS_PER_CLUSTER)
+/*
+ * 0x1000 shared by all cores, unless the platform sets SUNXI_PLAT_STACK_SIZE
+ * per core.
+ */
+#if defined(SUNXI_PLAT_STACK_SIZE) && (SUNXI_PLAT_STACK_SIZE != 0)
+#define PLATFORM_STACK_SIZE		SUNXI_PLAT_STACK_SIZE
+#else
 #define PLATFORM_STACK_SIZE		(0x1000 / PLATFORM_CORE_COUNT)
+#endif
 
 /* For the generic GIC driver (USE_GIC_DRIVER). */
 #define PLAT_ARM_GICD_BASE		SUNXI_GICD_BASE
diff --git a/plat/allwinner/common/include/sunxi_private.h b/plat/allwinner/common/include/sunxi_private.h
index 75216a7b4..b83e3acc9 100644
--- a/plat/allwinner/common/include/sunxi_private.h
+++ b/plat/allwinner/common/include/sunxi_private.h
@@ -24,6 +24,17 @@ void sunxi_cpu_power_off_others(void);
 void sunxi_cpu_power_off_self(void);
 void sunxi_power_down(void);
 
+#if SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND
+/*
+ * Provided per SoC, next to sunxi_cpu_power_off_self(): the registers that
+ * permit a core to power itself down are not the same on every Allwinner
+ * part, and on the A733 they are an Arm PPU that the SoC manual does not
+ * document.
+ */
+void sunxi_cpu_suspend_setup(uintptr_t sec_entrypoint);
+bool sunxi_cpu_can_suspend(unsigned int core);
+#endif
+
 #if SUNXI_PSCI_USE_NATIVE
 void sunxi_set_native_psci_ops(const plat_psci_ops_t **psci_ops);
 #else
diff --git a/plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_native_pm.c b/plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_native_pm.c
index 4c2a97a76..d36812c4e 100644
--- a/plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_native_pm.c
+++ b/plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_native_pm.c
@@ -4,6 +4,10 @@
  * SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
  */
 
+#include <assert.h>
+
+#include <platform_def.h>
+
 #include <arch_helpers.h>
 #include <common/debug.h>
 #if !USE_GIC_DRIVER
@@ -12,6 +16,7 @@
 #include <drivers/delay_timer.h>
 #include <lib/mmio.h>
 #include <lib/psci/psci.h>
+#include <plat/common/platform.h>
 
 #include <sunxi_mmap.h>
 #include <sunxi_private.h>
@@ -46,6 +51,110 @@ static void sunxi_pwr_domain_on_finish(const psci_power_state_t *target_state)
 #endif
 }
 
+#if SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND
+/*
+ * The only StateID this platform defines. The device tree encodes core
+ * power-down as 0x00010000: type POWERDOWN in bit 16, affinity level 0 in
+ * bits [25:24], StateID zero.
+ */
+#define SUNXI_STATEID_PWRDN		U(0)
+
+/*
+ * Native CPU_SUSPEND, core level.
+ *
+ * The state carries StateID 0 -- its meaning is in the power-level field,
+ * bits [25:24] -- so this decodes the level and NOT the StateID. A StateID
+ * pass-through validator (see sunxi_scpi_pm.c) would return all-RUN for
+ * StateID 0, psci_find_target_suspend_lvl() would answer
+ * PSCI_INVALID_PWR_LVL and psci_main.c would panic().
+ *
+ * A level the platform does not implement is refused, and the device tree
+ * must not declare it: Linux never retires a state the firmware refuses --
+ * cpuidle counts the rejection and the idle loop asks for the same state
+ * again at once, spinning through SMCs instead of sleeping.
+ */
+static int sunxi_validate_power_state(unsigned int power_state,
+				      psci_power_state_t *req_state)
+{
+	unsigned int pwr_lvl  = psci_get_pstate_pwrlvl(power_state);
+	unsigned int type     = psci_get_pstate_type(power_state);
+	unsigned int state_id = psci_get_pstate_id(power_state);
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	assert(req_state != NULL);
+
+	if (type != PSTATE_TYPE_POWERDOWN) {
+		return PSCI_E_INVALID_PARAMS;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Level 1 (the cluster) and level 2 (the system) are not
+	 * implemented. PLAT_MAX_PWR_LVL is 2 and the topology is root / one
+	 * cluster / the cores (sunxi_topology.c), so a level-1 request is
+	 * well-formed -- it is refused for what it asks, not for how it is
+	 * encoded.
+	 */
+	if (pwr_lvl != PSCI_CPU_PWR_LVL) {
+		return PSCI_E_INVALID_PARAMS;
+	}
+
+	if (state_id != SUNXI_STATEID_PWRDN) {
+		return PSCI_E_INVALID_PARAMS;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Hardware gate. If this core's power controller is not in the
+	 * configuration the down path assumes, refuse the state rather than
+	 * quiesce the GIC around a core that will not gate and then cannot be
+	 * woken. PSCI_E_DENIED would be more expressive, but psci_main.c
+	 * asserts that validate_power_state returns only INVALID_PARAMS.
+	 */
+	if (!sunxi_cpu_can_suspend(plat_my_core_pos())) {
+		return PSCI_E_INVALID_PARAMS;
+	}
+
+	for (i = PSCI_CPU_PWR_LVL; i <= PLAT_MAX_PWR_LVL; ++i) {
+		req_state->pwr_domain_state[i] = (i <= pwr_lvl) ?
+			PLAT_MAX_OFF_STATE : PSCI_LOCAL_STATE_RUN;
+	}
+
+	return PSCI_E_SUCCESS;
+}
+
+/*
+ * By the time this runs the PSCI library has set
+ * CPUPWRCTLR_EL1.CORE_PWRDN_EN (prepare_cpu_pwr_dwn()) and the generic GIC
+ * driver has run gicv3_cpuif_disable(): the GIC-600 TRM 3.6.2 core
+ * power-down sequence, ending in GICR_WAKER.ProcessorSleep = 1, which makes
+ * the redistributor assert wake_request for this core -- the only thing
+ * that brings it back. The redistributor stays powered (no
+ * gicv3_rdistif_off(), which is right for CPU_OFF and fatal here), and
+ * GIC_WAKEUP_DISABLE, which sunxi_cpu_off() sets for CPU_OFF, stays clear.
+ *
+ * No power-controller write: PWR_POLICY is OFF since setup and re-checked
+ * in sunxi_validate_power_state(). The core gates at the WFI that follows,
+ * which drops its P-Channel ON request.
+ */
+static void sunxi_pwr_domain_suspend(const psci_power_state_t *target_state)
+{
+	assert(is_local_state_off(
+		target_state->pwr_domain_state[PSCI_CPU_PWR_LVL]) != 0);
+	assert(is_local_state_run(
+		target_state->pwr_domain_state[PSCI_CPU_PWR_LVL + 1U]) != 0);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The generic GIC driver runs gicv3_cpuif_enable() after this, which clears
+ * GICR_WAKER.ProcessorSleep (and so drops wake_request) and restores the
+ * ICC_* system registers. It does not re-run gicv3_rdistif_init(), whose
+ * defaults would disable every SGI and PPI the OS had enabled.
+ */
+static void sunxi_pwr_domain_suspend_finish(
+		const psci_power_state_t *target_state)
+{
+}
+#endif /* SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND */
+
 static void sunxi_system_off(void)
 {
 #if !USE_GIC_DRIVER
@@ -137,6 +246,15 @@ static const plat_psci_ops_t sunxi_native_psci_ops = {
 	.pwr_domain_on			= sunxi_pwr_domain_on,
 	.pwr_domain_off			= sunxi_pwr_domain_off,
 	.pwr_domain_on_finish		= sunxi_pwr_domain_on_finish,
+#if SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND
+	/*
+	 * psci_setup.c grants PSCI_CPU_SUSPEND_AARCH64 only when all three of
+	 * these are present.
+	 */
+	.pwr_domain_suspend		= sunxi_pwr_domain_suspend,
+	.pwr_domain_suspend_finish	= sunxi_pwr_domain_suspend_finish,
+	.validate_power_state		= sunxi_validate_power_state,
+#endif
 	.system_off			= sunxi_system_off,
 	.system_reset			= sunxi_system_reset,
 	.validate_ns_entrypoint		= sunxi_validate_ns_entrypoint,
diff --git a/plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_pm.c b/plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_pm.c
index ebc406b91..ec7e3b20f 100644
--- a/plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_pm.c
+++ b/plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_pm.c
@@ -58,14 +58,36 @@ int plat_setup_psci_ops(uintptr_t sec_entrypoint,
 			mmio_write_32(SUNXI_ALT_RVBAR_HI_REG(cpu),
 				      sec_entrypoint >> 32);
 		}
+#ifdef SUNXI_CPUCFG_CPU_CTRL_REG
+		/*
+		 * The execution state the core resets into. RVBAR is the
+		 * AArch64 reset vector only; with AA64nAA32 = 0 a reset lands
+		 * in the BROM instead. sunxi_cpu_on() sets the bit for the core
+		 * it starts, but the boot core was released by the BROM/boot0
+		 * in AArch32 and switched with an RMR warm reset, which leaves
+		 * the bit at its reset value 0 -- so its first power-down wake
+		 * would run the BROM while the OS runs on. Set here for every
+		 * core, next to RVBAR, before any core can reset.
+		 */
+		mmio_setbits_32(SUNXI_CPUCFG_CPU_CTRL_REG(cpu),
+				SUNXI_CPUCFG_CPU_CTRL_AA64);
+#endif
 	}
 
 	if (sunxi_set_scpi_psci_ops(psci_ops) == 0) {
 		INFO("PSCI: Suspend is available via SCPI\n");
 		psci_is_scpi = true;
 	} else {
-		INFO("PSCI: Suspend is unavailable\n");
 		sunxi_set_native_psci_ops(psci_ops);
+#if SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND
+		/*
+		 * Arm the platform's own CPU_SUSPEND. Prints its own summary,
+		 * including which cores ended up able to offer the state.
+		 */
+		sunxi_cpu_suspend_setup(sec_entrypoint);
+#else
+		INFO("PSCI: Suspend is unavailable\n");
+#endif
 	}
 
 	return 0;
diff --git a/plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733/platform.mk b/plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733/platform.mk
index f70de3ad2..3b9e6e367 100644
--- a/plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733/platform.mk
+++ b/plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733/platform.mk
@@ -32,6 +32,18 @@ SUNXI_NEW_MPIDR_FMT := 1
 SUNXI_PSCI_USE_SCPI := 0
 SUNXI_PSCI_USE_NATIVE := 1
 
+# Native PSCI CPU_SUSPEND: core-level power-down through the per-core Arm
+# PPUs in the CPUIDLE block, with no SCP (sunxi_cpu_suspend.c).
+SUNXI_PSCI_NATIVE_SUSPEND := 1
+
+# EL3 stack per core. The common default, 0x1000 shared by eight cores =
+# 512 B each, is not enough for the CPU_SUSPEND path as soon as anything on
+# it prints (gcc -fcallgraph-info=su on the link-time optimised release
+# build, deepest chain from std_svc_smc_handler: 928 B). The stacks are
+# contiguous, so an overflow silently corrupts the next core's frames.
+# 2 KiB, in the NOBITS region.
+SUNXI_PLAT_STACK_SIZE := 2048
+
 # GIC-600, driven by the generic GICv3 driver.
 USE_GIC_DRIVER := 3
 GICV3_SUPPORT_GIC600 := 1
diff --git a/plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733/sunxi_cpu_suspend.c b/plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733/sunxi_cpu_suspend.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..56423f30b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733/sunxi_cpu_suspend.c
@@ -0,0 +1,233 @@
+/*
+ * Copyright (c) 2026, DockSeed. All rights reserved.
+ *
+ * SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
+ */
+
+/*
+ * A733 (sun60iw2) CPU-subsystem PPU access for native PSCI CPU_SUSPEND.
+ *
+ * WHAT THIS BLOCK IS. The A733 UM memory map calls 0x07050000-0x0705FFFF
+ * (64K, SUNXI_R_CPUCFG_BASE) "CPUIDLE" (the block it calls "PPU" is a
+ * different instance at 0x07060000, the peripheral PCK-600). The UM
+ * documents no registers in the CPUIDLE block. What the block contains, read
+ * on silicon, is Arm PPU components that identify themselves:
+ *
+ *   CIDR = 0xB105F00D, PIDR part 0x0B6 / JEP106 0x43B = Arm,
+ *   IIDR = 0x0B61143B, AIDR = 0x11 -> PPU architecture v1.1
+ *
+ * Every register field below therefore comes from Arm DEN0051E (PPU
+ * Architecture Specification v1.1), and every value is checked against the
+ * instance's own PPU_IDR0 before it is relied on.
+ *
+ * WHICH INSTANCE IS WHICH. Proven by stimulus, not inferred from order:
+ * taking cpu3 offline moves only 0x07055008 and cpu7 only 0x07059008, both
+ * reversibly, while 0x07051008 never moves. So the DSU PPU is at +0x1000 and
+ * core N is at +0x2000 + N * 0x1000, as the hotplug code in
+ * sunxi_cpu_new_ops.c has it.
+ *
+ * WHAT THIS FILE DOES, AND WHY SO LITTLE. Cores 1-7 already sit at
+ * PWR_POLICY = OFF with PWR_DYN_EN = 1, i.e. the hardware is already allowed
+ * to gate them when they ask; suspend needs no write for them at all. Core 0
+ * was released by boot0 and never went through sunxi_cpu_on(), so it reads
+ * PWR_POLICY = ON -- and with dynamic transitions enabled the policy is the
+ * MINIMUM power mode (DEN0051E 5.2.1), so core 0 can never gate. That
+ * asymmetry is the only thing this file writes.
+ *
+ * The same history leaves core 0 a second asymmetry: CLU0_CPU0_CTRL_REG
+ * AA64nAA32 = 0 (sunxi_cpu_on() sets it only for the core it starts), so a
+ * reset of core 0 lands in AArch32 at the BROM instead of at RVBAR. It is
+ * set for every core in plat_setup_psci_ops() (sunxi_pm.c), next to RVBAR;
+ * this file only reads both back and refuses the state to a core whose
+ * reset would not land in BL31.
+ */
+
+#include <stdbool.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+
+#include <platform_def.h>
+
+#include <common/debug.h>
+#include <lib/cassert.h>
+#include <lib/mmio.h>
+#include <lib/utils_def.h>
+#include <plat/common/platform.h>
+
+#include <sunxi_cpucfg.h>
+#include <sunxi_mmap.h>
+#include <sunxi_private.h>
+
+/* PPU instance apertures inside the CPUIDLE block, from +0x2000 on. */
+#define A733_PPU_CPU(n)			(SUNXI_R_CPUCFG_BASE + U(0x2000) + \
+					 (n) * U(0x1000))
+
+/* The whole map has to stay inside the 64K the UM gives the block. */
+CASSERT(A733_PPU_CPU(PLATFORM_CORE_COUNT - 1) + U(0xFFF) <
+	SUNXI_R_CPUCFG_BASE + U(0x10000),
+	assert_a733_ppu_map_fits_cpuidle_block);
+
+/* Arm PPU register offsets, DEN0051E Table 5-1. */
+#define PPU_PWPR			U(0x000)
+#define PPU_IDR0			U(0xFB0)
+
+/* DEN0051E Table 5-2 (PWPR). */
+#define PPU_PWR_POLICY_MASK		U(0xF)		/* PWPR [3:0] */
+#define PPU_PWR_DYN_EN			BIT_32(8)	/* PWPR [8]   */
+
+/*
+ * DEN0051E Table 3-2, and independently the A733 UM v0.92 Table 5-2
+ * (its table of the Cortex-A76 power modes), which prints OFF as 0b000000.
+ */
+#define PPU_MODE_OFF			U(0x0)
+
+/* DEN0051E Table 5-26. */
+#define PPU_IDR0_STA_OFF_SPT		BIT_32(8)
+#define PPU_IDR0_DYN_OFF_SPT		BIT_32(20)
+
+/*
+ * This code decodes the original PSCI power-state format: the DT declares
+ * 0x00010000, whose StateID field is zero and whose meaning is carried by
+ * bits [25:24]. PSCI_EXTENDED_STATE_ID is 0 by default
+ * (make_helpers/defaults.mk) and no Allwinner platform overrides it. Fail the
+ * build rather than silently misread the parameter if that ever changes.
+ */
+#if PSCI_EXTENDED_STATE_ID
+#error "sun60i_a733 native suspend decodes the original power-state format"
+#endif
+
+/*
+ * Per-core, set once at setup: does this core's PPU instance report that OFF
+ * is supported at all, statically and as a dynamic transition. An instance
+ * that is not implemented reads as zero and fails this, which is the answer
+ * we want.
+ */
+static bool cpu_ppu_off_capable[PLATFORM_CORE_COUNT];
+
+/*
+ * Per-core, set once at setup: does a reset of this core land in BL31's warm
+ * entry point. A core the PPU turns back on after a power-down starts at its
+ * reset vector, and that is RVBARADDR only in AArch64 state
+ * (CLU0_CPUn_CTRL_REG.AA64nAA32 = 1, sunxi_cpucfg_ncat3.h). Both are
+ * programmed for every core by plat_setup_psci_ops() just before setup and
+ * read back here. With the bit at 0, the boot core's first power-down wake
+ * ran the BROM and boot0 in the middle of the running kernel.
+ */
+static bool cpu_resets_to_bl31[PLATFORM_CORE_COUNT];
+
+/*
+ * True when this core's PPU is in the exact configuration the suspend path
+ * assumes. Called on every CPU_SUSPEND, before anything is committed.
+ *
+ * One MMIO read. PWPR[8] is read rather than PWSR[8] deliberately: DEN0051E
+ * notes a dynamic-enable write may take time to appear in PWSR, but we never
+ * write that bit, and PWPR is the register whose PWR_POLICY we have to read
+ * anyway.
+ */
+bool sunxi_cpu_can_suspend(unsigned int core)
+{
+	uint32_t pwpr;
+
+	if ((core >= PLATFORM_CORE_COUNT) || !cpu_ppu_off_capable[core] ||
+	    !cpu_resets_to_bl31[core]) {
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	pwpr = mmio_read_32(A733_PPU_CPU(core) + PPU_PWPR);
+
+	/*
+	 * With PWR_DYN_EN clear, PWR_POLICY is the TARGET power mode rather
+	 * than the minimum (DEN0051E 5.2.1) and this whole model does not
+	 * apply -- a core in that state must not be offered the idle state.
+	 */
+	if ((pwpr & PPU_PWR_DYN_EN) == 0U) {
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Anything above OFF is a floor this core cannot go below, so it will
+	 * not gate. Offering the state anyway would mean setting
+	 * GICR_WAKER.ProcessorSleep on a core that stays ON, which the GIC-600
+	 * TRM (3.6.2) forbids and which leaves the core unwakeable in WFI.
+	 */
+	return (pwpr & PPU_PWR_POLICY_MASK) == PPU_MODE_OFF;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Called once from plat_setup_psci_ops(), on the boot core, before any
+ * secondary has been started.
+ *
+ * Reads are safe on a core that has never run: the PPU is the controller, not
+ * the controlled domain, and sunxi_cpu_on() polls PPU_PWSR of an off core.
+ *
+ * The write is deliberately confined to the calling core. Cores 1-7 are
+ * measured to reach PWR_POLICY = OFF by themselves once sunxi_cpu_on() has
+ * run, so writing their policy here -- before they have ever been started,
+ * in a state nobody has measured -- would risk SMP bring-up to fix something
+ * that is not broken. If a secondary ever does come up with a different
+ * policy, sunxi_cpu_can_suspend() refuses it the idle state; it does not hang
+ * it.
+ */
+void sunxi_cpu_suspend_setup(uintptr_t sec_entrypoint)
+{
+	unsigned int self = plat_my_core_pos();
+	unsigned int core;
+	unsigned int mask = 0U;
+	unsigned int reset_ok = 0U;
+	uint32_t before, after;
+	uintptr_t ppu;
+
+	for (core = 0U; core < PLATFORM_CORE_COUNT; ++core) {
+		uint32_t idr0 = mmio_read_32(A733_PPU_CPU(core) + PPU_IDR0);
+		uint32_t need = PPU_IDR0_STA_OFF_SPT | PPU_IDR0_DYN_OFF_SPT;
+
+		cpu_ppu_off_capable[core] = ((idr0 & need) == need);
+
+		cpu_resets_to_bl31[core] =
+			((mmio_read_32(SUNXI_CPUCFG_CPU_CTRL_REG(core)) &
+			  SUNXI_CPUCFG_CPU_CTRL_AA64) != 0U) &&
+			(mmio_read_32(SUNXI_CPUCFG_RVBAR_LO_REG(core)) ==
+			 (uint32_t)sec_entrypoint) &&
+			(mmio_read_32(SUNXI_CPUCFG_RVBAR_HI_REG(core)) ==
+			 (uint32_t)(sec_entrypoint >> 32));
+		if (cpu_resets_to_bl31[core]) {
+			reset_ok |= BIT_32(core);
+		}
+	}
+
+	ppu = A733_PPU_CPU(self);
+	before = mmio_read_32(ppu + PPU_PWPR);
+	after = before;
+
+	if (cpu_ppu_off_capable[self] && cpu_resets_to_bl31[self] &&
+	    ((before & PPU_PWR_POLICY_MASK) != PPU_MODE_OFF)) {
+		/*
+		 * Read-modify-write: PWR_DYN_EN, LOCK_EN, OP_POLICY and
+		 * OP_DYN_EN are preserved. The value written is the one seven
+		 * of the eight cores already carry continuously on this
+		 * silicon, and hotplug works in both directions through it.
+		 */
+		mmio_write_32(ppu + PPU_PWPR,
+			      (before & ~PPU_PWR_POLICY_MASK) | PPU_MODE_OFF);
+		/*
+		 * Read back. DEN0051E: programming an unsupported policy makes
+		 * the PPU revert PWPR. Never assume the write took.
+		 */
+		after = mmio_read_32(ppu + PPU_PWPR);
+	}
+
+	for (core = 0U; core < PLATFORM_CORE_COUNT; ++core) {
+		if (sunxi_cpu_can_suspend(core)) {
+			mask |= BIT_32(core);
+		}
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * One line at every cold boot: which cores will actually offer the
+	 * state (0xff expected), the boot core's policy write, and which cores
+	 * reset into BL31 (AArch64, RVBAR = warm entry; 0xff expected, a core
+	 * missing from it is refused the state). NOTICE, so that a release
+	 * build (LOG_LEVEL 20) prints it.
+	 */
+	NOTICE("PSCI: native CPU_SUSPEND armed 0x%02x, core %u PWPR %08x->%08x, reset to BL31 0x%02x\n",
+	       mask, self, before, after, reset_ok);
+}
